# A NEON loop-restoration tier, and the tile-aware fix for the single-tile shift

date:   2026-08-08
host:   Apple M4 Pro (8 P-core + 4 E-core), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
build:  stable rustc, `--release`, DEFAULT features (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16,
        CHECKED DisjointMut). No `-C target-cpu=native`. No `nice` on any timed
        run (Darwin maps a niced process to background QoS; ~40x distortion).
base:   verify/compose-2 @ 2e0ea4d (PR #454), branch perf/lr-neon-tier
raw:    benchmarks/lr_neon_tier_2026-08-08_lr.tsv.zst   (sweeps
          lr_v1_vs_scalar, lr_v1_vs_v2, lr_v2_vs_scalar)
        benchmarks/lr_neon_tier_2026-08-08_gap.tsv.zst  (sweeps
          tilegate, tilegate_dav1d)

Every cell below was committed only when a whole rotated round completed with
ZERO foreign processes over 25% CPU; a sibling agent's benchmark was resident
on the box for part of the session and those rounds were discarded and rerun
(`scripts/perf/lr_neon_ab.sh` and `scripts/perf/verify_gap.sh` both do this
automatically and log every discard).


## PART 1 — the loop-restoration NEON tier

### The measurement trap this had to get past

`v4k_8tile` and `v4k_8tile_10b` — the two vectors the entire gap-to-dav1d table
is built on — do **ZERO loop restoration**. Every LR number in that campaign is
0.0 ms because of the bitstreams, not because of the kernel. LR is active in
**696 of 768** dav1d-test-data vectors (`md5_inventory --activity`, needs
`--features __ablate`). So this tier CANNOT move the gap grid, and no claim
here is made about it.

The A/B is therefore run on vectors where LR actually executes, chosen as the
corpus extremes and two mid-range cases:

  | vector | LR pixels | LR as % of all counted kernel pixels | frames |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | `8-bit/data/00001147`   | 33.4 M | 42.6% | 3 |
  | `10-bit/issues/318_tx_4x4` | 14.6 M | **76.1%** | 35 |
  | `8-bit/data/00000645`   | 14.6 M | 40.4% | 140 |
  | `8-bit/data/00000855`   |  1.5 M | 70.0% | 4 |

### Arms

Same binary on both arms, `__ablate` switching only which implementation the
dispatcher routes to, so codegen and layout cannot confound it:

  neon   = `safe_simd/looprestoration_arm.rs` (this tier)
  scalar = `src/looprestoration.rs`           (the generic reference)

`scripts/perf/lr_neon_ab.sh`, arm order rotated per round, whole round
committed or discarded as a unit, median of 5.

### Result — whole-frame decode, ms/frame, SHIPPED tier (sweep lr_v2_vs_scalar)

    vector                        neon    scalar   ratio   neon range         scalar range
    8-bit/data/00001147        172.218  196.704  0.8755   [171.66..173.65]   [195.67..197.26]
    10-bit/issues/318_tx_4x4     9.121    9.693  0.9410   [  9.11..  9.29]   [  9.64..  9.71]
    8-bit/data/00000855         26.334   26.627  0.9890   [ 26.32.. 26.36]   [ 26.62.. 26.72]
    8-bit/data/00000645          5.568    5.594  0.9952   [  5.55..  5.65]   [  5.58..  5.62]

Three of the four are real: their arms' ranges are disjoint. **`00000645` is
NOT** — [5.5456..5.6476] against [5.5792..5.6218] overlap, so 0.9952 is not
resolvable and must not be quoted as a delta. (In the earlier v1 sweep the same
cell read 0.9856 with disjoint ranges, which is the honest summary: it is a
small win of order 1%, and this sweep could not separate it.)

These are WHOLE-FRAME decode ratios, not kernel-local ones. On `00001147` loop
restoration is 42.6% of counted kernel pixels and the tier takes 12.4% off the
entire frame; on `318_tx_4x4`, where LR is 76.1% of kernel pixels but the frame
is 9 ms, it takes 5.9%.

### Two versions were measured, and the second one is what ships

v1 keeps the reference's shape: every restoration unit declares its scratch as
zero-initialised stack arrays (`tmp`, `hor`, `sumsq`, `sum`, one or two `dst`),
which is ~285 KB memset per 8bpc `sgr_mix` call and ~460 KB at 16bpc. That is
why `_platform_memset` was 6.7% of decode self time on `318_tx_4x4`.

v2 moves the scratch to a lazily-allocated per-thread buffer that is NEVER
cleared, because every position these kernels read is written first in the same
call (the argument is spelled out in the module header). See the correctness
section for how that is checked rather than asserted.

    vector                          v2       v1    v2/v1   v2 range          v1 range
    8-bit/data/00001147        172.888  176.644   0.9787  [172.15..173.83]  [175.92..177.24]
    8-bit/data/00000645          5.567    5.646   0.9860  [  5.54..  5.61]  [  5.62..  5.69]
    8-bit/data/00000855         26.381   26.460   0.9970  [ 26.33.. 26.39]  [ 26.44.. 26.52]
    10-bit/issues/318_tx_4x4     9.174    9.202   0.9970  [  9.14..  9.21]  [  9.18..  9.33]

The first three have disjoint ranges. The fourth does NOT — and it is the
vector whose profile named `_platform_memset` at 6.7% in the first place. So:
removing that memset is worth ~2% on the big 8bpc vector and is **not
resolvable on `318_tx_4x4`**. Reported rather than rounded away.

### For scale: what was here before

The module this replaces was 1,531 lines of hand-written SCALAR that
`lr_filter_dispatch` ran unconditionally at both bit depths. Measured
2026-08-07 the same way (`benchmarks/lr_arm_vs_reference_2026-08-07.meta`), it
was **1.060** on `00001147` — 6% SLOWER than the reference it shadowed.


## PART 2 — the adaptive block shift now sees the tile split

`verify/compose-2` recorded exactly one regression: `v4k_1tile` at t=8 cost
**+3.08%** from the tracker branch (362.7 -> 373.8 ms/frame, non-overlapping
per-round ranges) while `v4k_8tile` at t=8 GAINED 39% (117.3 -> 71.3) from the
same change at the same thread count. `block_shift_for` sizes itself from
buffer length and reads one concurrency fact, `set_parallelism(n_tc)`, so it
cannot tell those two cells apart.

Fix: a second monotone latch, `set_tile_concurrency(tiling.cols * rows)`,
declared in `rav1d_submit_frame` before that frame's picture is allocated
(the deadline, since the shift is read once in `BorrowTracker::new`). The
adaptive shift now requires threads AND more than one tile.

### Result — `scripts/perf/verify_gap.sh`, 2 arms interleaved, median of 5

`pre` = this branch minus the tile gate; `post` = with it. Both binaries carry
the LR tier, which is inert here (these vectors do no LR at all), so this
isolates the gate.

Sweep `tilegate` (2 arms, cells at t=4 and t=8):

    vector       t     pre     post   post/pre  pre per-round                        post per-round
    v4k_1tile    4   383.61   362.83    0.9458  [382.5 382.7 383.6 385.1 385.3]      [360.6 361.4 362.8 363.3 363.3]
    v4k_1tile    8   383.72   362.28    0.9441  [369.4 382.9 383.7 384.6 386.4]      [352.5 362.1 362.3 363.9 368.9]
    v4k_8tile    4   120.06   121.33    1.0106  [119.3 119.9 120.1 120.4 121.2]      [119.8 120.1 121.3 121.7 122.1]
    v4k_8tile    8    72.83    72.78    0.9992  [ 72.0  72.3  72.8  73.2  73.4]      [ 72.7  72.7  72.8  73.1  73.2]

Sweep `tilegate_dav1d` — an independent repeat with dav1d 1.5.4 measured in the
SAME interleave, `--framedelay 1` (the tile-threading-only model we implement):

    vector       t     pre     post   dav1d   post/dav1d   pre/dav1d
    v4k_1tile    8   382.89   362.39  240.17     1.51        1.59
    v4k_8tile    8    72.61    73.28   34.39     2.13        2.11

      pre  [382.3 382.8 382.9 384.0 385.1]   post [361.6 362.3 362.4 365.7 367.1]
      pre  [ 71.8  72.3  72.6  72.9  74.4]   post [ 73.1  73.1  73.3  73.4  74.3]

`v4k_1tile` t=8 is fixed, and it reproduced in two independent sweeps with
disjoint per-round ranges both times: 0.9441 and 0.9465. The gap to dav1d on
that cell goes **1.59x -> 1.51x**, which is the best t=8 ratio anywhere in this
campaign.

`v4k_8tile` t=8 is the cell that had to NOT break, and it does not: 0.9992 in
one sweep, 1.0092 in the other, arms' ranges overlapping in both. Null. (For
orientation: verify/compose-2 measured 73.1 composed there, and this branch's
post arm reads 72.8 / 73.3.)

`v4k_8tile` t=4 reads 1.0106 but its arms' ranges overlap too (pre
119.33..121.17, post 119.83..122.11), so it is likewise not resolvable and is
not claimed as a regression. The gate is inert for an 8-tile frame by
construction — `tiles = 8 > 1` selects exactly the behaviour `pre` had.


## CORRECTNESS

Two independent gates, both run on the SHIPPED tree, both proved live by
mutation rather than assumed.

**Per call, over the whole corpus.** `--features __simd_test_log` re-runs the
scalar reference after every loop-restoration call and compares the `w`x`h`
output region.

    768 vectors, 766 PASS / 2 SKIP / 0 mismatch / 0 error, ZERO LR_MISMATCH

**Per vector.** `examples/md5_inventory` set-diffed BY NAME against
`benchmarks/aarch64_md5_fixes_2026-08-07_final.tsv.zst` with the ACTUAL md5 in
the key — identical on all 768 rows, for v1 and again for v2.

**Variant coverage is proved, not assumed.** The new measurement-only
`__lrvarcov` feature prints each (bit depth, variant) pair the first time it is
dispatched. The corpus run printed all TEN:

    8bpc  wiener7  wiener5  sgr_5x5  sgr_3x3  sgr_mix
    16bpc wiener7  wiener5  sgr_5x5  sgr_3x3  sgr_mix

**The differential is live.** A `+1` planted in the 8bpc Wiener horizontal bias
produced 996 `LR_MISMATCH` lines (variants wiener5, wiener7) and flipped
`00001147` to MISMATCH. Weights planted in `six_i32` / `mid_i32` / `eight_i32`
— which exist ONLY on the vector path — produced `LR_MISMATCH` on sgr_5x5 and
sgr_mix across 8-, 10- and 12-bit vectors (145 / 12 / 36 lines). Restored,
green.

**The never-cleared scratch does not read before it writes.** `--features
__lrpoison` fills every scratch buffer with a non-zero pattern before each
call, so any read-before-write becomes a divergence. Full corpus under poison:
**766 PASS / 0 mismatch / 0 LR_MISMATCH**. That instrument is live too: with a
band of columns deliberately left unwritten, the poisoned build and the
un-poisoned build both fail — with DIFFERENT md5s (066dc570... vs 8c1ad66f...),
which is only possible if the poison pattern is reaching the output.

**The tracker's own detection still fires.** M1 — the 4af62ae TOCTOU, i.e.
deleting the in-lock `state` re-read from `add`'s single-block fast path — was
planted ON TOP of the tile-gate change:
`wide_exclusion::a_wide_borrow_excludes_every_narrow_shard` FAILED in 0.01 s
with 135 violations. Restored, green. 25 tracker unit tests + `wide_exclusion`
+ doctests pass.


## WHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY

* It says nothing about the gap-to-dav1d grid from PART 1. Those two vectors do
  no loop restoration; the table cannot move and was not re-measured for it.
* The four A/B vectors are the corpus extremes by LR share plus two mid-range
  cases. No claim is made about the median of the 696 LR-active vectors.
* Absolute ms/frame here are NOT comparable to the verify/compose-2 campaign's
  (this session's `pre` arm reads ~4% higher on v4k_1tile than that campaign's
  `composed`). Only the paired, same-sweep ratios are.
* The tier is NEON baseline only. No dotprod/i8mm, no SVE.


## REPRODUCING

    # activity counters: which vectors run LR at all
    cargo build --release --features __ablate --example md5_inventory
    ./target/release/examples/md5_inventory --activity > activity.tsv

    # per-call bit-exactness over the corpus (slow: ~25x)
    cargo build --release --features __simd_test_log,__lrvarcov --example md5_inventory
    ./target/release/examples/md5_inventory > /dev/null 2> mismatches.txt

    # same, with the reused scratch poisoned
    cargo build --release --features __simd_test_log,__lrpoison --example md5_inventory

    # the A/B
    cargo build --release --features __ablate --example profile_ivf
    BIN=./target/release/examples/profile_ivf scripts/perf/lr_neon_ab.sh out.tsv 5

    # the tile gate
    BIN=<staged bins> ARMS="pre post" CELLS="v4k_1tile:8 v4k_8tile:8" \
      scripts/perf/verify_gap.sh tilegate.tsv 5
